1
00:00:02,973 --> 00:00:07,581
(Q21&22（多选）) [展览任务与目标观众] Cathy: OK, Graham, so let's check we both know what we're supposed to be doing. Cathy：

2
00:00:07,581 --> 00:00:08,915
Graham: OK. Graham：

3
00:00:08,915 --> 00:00:13,945
Cathy: So, for the university's open day, we have to plan a display on British life Cathy：

4
00:00:13,945 --> 00:00:16,559
and literature in the mid-19th century.

5
00:00:16,559 --> 00:00:17,376
Graham: That's right. Graham：

6
00:00:17,376 --> 00:00:24,160
But we'll have some people to help us find the materials and set it up,remember. for the moment, we just need to plan it.

7
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:29,255
Cathy:Good. So have you gathered who's expected to come and see the display? Cathy：

8
00:00:29,255 --> 00:00:33,912
Is it for the people studying English, or students from other departments?

9
00:00:33,912 --> 00:00:35,261
I'm not clear about it.

10
00:00:35,261 --> 00:00:36,313
Graham:Nor me. Graham：

11
00:00:36,313 --> 00:00:46,424
That was how it used to be, but it didn't attract many people, so this year it's going to be part of an open day, to raise the university's profile.

12
00:00:46,424 --> 00:00:53,011
It'll be publicized in the city, to encourage people to come and find out something of what goes on here.

13
00:00:53,011 --> 00:00:59,744
And it's included in the information that's sent to people who are considering applying to study here next year.

14
00:00:59,744 --> 00:01:03,437
Cathy:Presumably some current students and lecturers will come? Cathy：

15
00:01:03,437 --> 00:01:08,747
Graham:I would imagine so, but we've been told to concentrate on the other categories of people. Graham：

16
00:01:08,747 --> 00:01:14,029
[以狄更斯作品切入社会生活] Cathy:Right. We don't have to cover the whole range of 19th-century literature, do we? Cathy：

17
00:01:14,029 --> 00:01:19,843
Graham:No, it's entirely up to us. I suggest just using Charles Dickens. Graham：

18
00:01:19,843 --> 00:01:25,712
Cathy:That's a good idea. Most people have heard of him, and have probably read some of his novels, Cathy：

19
00:01:25,712 --> 00:01:31,324
or seen films based on them, so that's a good lead-in to life in his time.

20
00:01:31,324 --> 00:01:36,952
Graham:Exactly. And his novels show the awful conditions that most people had to live in, don't they: Graham：

21
00:01:36,952 --> 00:01:40,233
he wanted to shock people into doing something about it.

22
00:01:40,233 --> 00:01:43,627
Cathy:Did he do any campaigning, other than writing? Cathy：

23
00:01:43,627 --> 00:01:48,361
Graham:Yes, he campaigned for education and other social reforms, Graham：

24
00:01:48,361 --> 00:01:53,264
and gave talks, but I'm inclined to ignore that and focus on the novels.

25
00:01:53,264 --> 00:01:58,259
Cathy:Yes, I agree. Cathy：

26
00:01:58,259 --> 00:02:03,369
(Q25（Matching）) Cathy:OK, so now shall we think about a topic linked to each novel? Cathy：

27
00:02:03,369 --> 00:02:12,175
Graham:Yes. I've printed out a list of Dickens's novels in the order they were published, in the hope you'd agree to focus on him!

28
00:02:12,175 --> 00:02:16,091
Cathy:You're lucky I did agree! Let's have a look.&nbsp;

29
00:02:16,091 --> 00:02:22,956
OK, the first was The Pickwick Papers, published in 1836.

30
00:02:22,956 --> 00:02:28,893
It was very successful when it came out, wasn't it, and was adapted for the theatre straight away.

31
00:02:28,893 --> 00:02:45,784
[Q25答案句（G） medical condition = medicine 平行替换，词性] Graham:There's an interesting point, though, that there's a character who keeps falling asleep, and that medical condition was named after the book – Pickwickian Syndrome.Cathy:Oh, so why don't we use that as the topic, and include some quotations from the novel?&nbsp;Graham:Right.

32
00:02:45,784 --> 00:02:51,491
(Q26) Graham：Next is Oliver Twist. There's a lot in the novel about poverty.&nbsp;

33
00:02:51,491 --> 00:02:53,589
But maybe something less obvious …

34
00:02:53,589 --> 00:03:08,696
[Q26答案句（B） 解释型] Cathy:Well Oliver is taught how to steal, isn't he?&nbsp; We could use that to illustrate the fact that very few children went to school,particularly not poor children, so they learnt in other ways.Graham:Good idea.&nbsp;

35
00:03:08,696 --> 00:03:10,214
(Q27) Graham：What's next?&nbsp;

36
00:03:10,214 --> 00:03:16,707
Cathy:Maybe Nicholas Nickleby. Actually he taught in a really cruel school, didn't he? Cathy：

37
00:03:16,707 --> 00:03:22,798
Graham:That's right. But there's also the company of touring actors that Nicholas joins.&nbsp;

38
00:03:22,798 --> 00:03:31,935
[Q27答案句（D） 平行替换 amusement = entertainment] Graham：We could do something on theaters and other amusements of the time. We don't want only the bad things, do we?Cathy:OK.&nbsp;

39
00:03:31,935 --> 00:03:36,853
(Q28) Graham:What about Martin Chuzzlewit? He goes to the USA, doesn't he? Graham：

40
00:03:36,853 --> 00:03:44,382
[Q28答案句C：（一人提出）] Cathy:Yes, and Dickens himself had been there a year before, and drew on his experience there in the novel.

41
00:03:44,382 --> 00:03:58,334
[Q28答案句 另一人提出另一个，随即否定， 说用你的吧还是。] Graham:I wonder, though … The main theme is selfishness, so we could do something on social justice?No, too general, let's keep to your idea – I think it would work well.

42
00:03:58,334 --> 00:04:07,220
(Q29) Cathy:He wrote Bleak House next – that's my favourite of his novels. Graham:Yes, mine too. His satire of the legal system is pretty powerful.

43
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:30,316
[Q29答案句（H） a woman's life] Cathy:That's true, but think about Esther, the heroine. As a child she lives with someone she doesn't know is her aunt, who treats her very badly.Then she's very happy living with her guardian, and he puts her in charge of the household.And at the end she gets married and her guardian gives her and her husband a house, where of course they're very happy.Graham:Yes, I like that.&nbsp;

44
00:04:30,316 --> 00:04:34,610
(Q30) Cathy:What shall we take next? Little Dorrit? Cathy：

45
00:04:34,610 --> 00:04:38,347
Old Mr Dorrit has been in a debtors' prison for years …

46
00:04:38,347 --> 00:04:40,844
Graham:So was Dickens's father, wasn't he? Graham：

47
00:04:40,844 --> 00:04:42,128
Cathy:That's right. Cathy：

48
00:04:42,128 --> 00:04:52,048
[Q30答案句（F）： 解释 继承财产+假装rick = wealth] Graham:What about focusing on the part when Mr Dorrit inherits a fortune, and he starts pretending he's always been rich?Cathy:Good idea.&nbsp;

49
00:04:52,048 --> 00:04:57,899
Graham:OK, so next we need to think about what materials we want to illustrate each issue. Graham：

50
00:04:57,899 --> 00:05:00,399
That's going to be quite hard.

